 points either way"], "rationale": "No New Jersey general-election polls or expert ratings have been published, so the forecast rests on the state's four-point Democratic lean; with the seat open and nominees unset, there is no specific evidence to move the expected margin away from that lean.", "watch": "The first credible New Jersey polls and the identity of the actual nominees — particularly whether Chris Christie runs — could shift the state several points in either direction.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "New Jersey enters the 2028 presidential race as a modest Democratic-leaning state, with a partisan lean of about four points Democratic and no incumbent president on the ballot due to term limits. No general-election polls of New Jersey or expert ratings are available yet, so the outlook rests on the state's underlying partisanship.\n\nHeadline coverage so far concerns national 2028 primary speculation — Newsom, Harris and Ocasio-Cortez among Democrats; Vance, Rubio and a possible third bid by former New Jersey governor Chris Christie among Republicans — none of it specific to how the state will vote in November. With the nominees unset and polling absent, uncertainty is wide, and a strong national swing in either direction could put New Jersey's fourteen electoral votes in play.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6761, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.642, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6472, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6427, "margin": 4.0}]}